+# Execution ownership consumers — source preparation
+
+[中文](./execution-ownership-consumers.zh-CN.md)
+
+This consumer delta is prepared after the stable patches 1–6 plus the `df21a34` ownership checkpoint. It is not
+an activated patch or a final 1–10 composition. The dispatcher bootstrap still refuses registration; no Native,
+CLI, service, LSP, browser, attachment or cleanup runtime capability has been demonstrated by this delta.
+
+`createAgentSession` and the legacy factory await `initializeExecutionOwnership()` as their first body action,
+before inference registration or resource-loader access. SDK eager imports are not claimed to be pure. Every Bun
+worker realm still needs independently authenticated process registration through the public context.
+
+All ordinary project/global daemon clients acquire the fixed public dispatcher grant before construction. They no
+longer create/read a broker token, create its runtime directory, invoke private broker worker argv, or locally spawn
+on initial connection/reconnect. Cached factories, requests, cancel requests and completion acknowledgements obtain
+fresh grants. A changed scope generation, runtime directory, token, dispatcher or release/schema closes that client
+and throws a typed ownership failure. There is no automatic attach to a replacement or legacy broker. The broker
+smoke function now probes the independently selected broker; it does not claim to exercise a client-owned spawn.
+
+`DaemonBrokerClient.runtimeDir` is authoritative for service logs, IDA/blob/LSP child sockets, text-prediction
+endpoints, shared Chromium profiles and durable target registries. Consumers do not derive those paths from the
+profile or project directory. Legacy `runtimeDir` overrides must equal the grant; `idleGraceMs` overrides are refused.
+The only global selectors accepted here are Native's `browser-relay` and `text-predict`; this is not a browser argv API.
+
+Named shared bash service operations, browser tool invocation/acquisition/release/health replacement, relay probes
+and replacement, IDA acquisition/socket requests, and prediction factories/requests/sync fail before shared effects
+when `requireSharedExecution()` refuses. Prediction refuses before SmolLM weight prefetch and old-daemon shutdown.
+The native error code remains an error; no synthetic tool-success or unsupported capability flag is returned.
+Generic builtin bash, private background execution and native parent-owned subagents are not changed here.
+
+A **registered private** LSP client bypasses both automatic external lspmux wrapping and the shared transport before
+spawning the existing private process transport. An unavailable context throws before choosing that fallback. A
+registered private blob consumer returns `null` before shared-daemon factories and uses the existing in-process
+`LocalBlobBackend`; unavailable context is not interpreted as private or external. No image/document conversion,
+attachment limit or consumption guarantee is changed or proven by these edits.
+
+Validation in this preparation is limited to the existing public ownership decision/wire tests under a synthetic
+allowlisted environment with the egress guard preloaded, source compiler comparison, and patch/source identities.
+No new consumer behavior has runtime red/green evidence yet. Required later evidence includes private and nested
+`env -i` creation/reconnect denial, external TUI/service survival, first-action SDK refusal, actual private LSP,
+every mandatory image/text/document format and budget, cancellation, background/subagent behavior, and owned cleanup.
+Existing broker and browser tests that bootstrap local brokers or fabricate old scope objects need migration to the
+same authenticated public dispatcher fixture before they can validate this changed installation contract.
+
+Linux is the current delivery target; actual Windows Chrome/WSL2 remains owner-deferred. This policy does not sandbox
+same-UID code, arbitrary downloaded old Native, or irreversible external tool effects.
